<node id="RAG Second Brain Paper">
  <data key="d0">RAG Second Brain Paper</data>
  <data key="d1">content</data>
  <data key="d2">The RAG Second Brain Paper is an active academic research paper and project document that proposes a multi-source Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG) framework, specifically focusing on the application of multi-source knowledge graph (KG) RAG within the field of medical diagnosis. Its proposed methodology integrates dense vector retrieval with co-occurrence-based statistical retrieval, utilizing Pointwise Mutual Information (PPMI), and is broadly connected to RAG and "Second Brain" concepts. This primary academic paper, created by the Academic Team, is central to work log activities, is related to the broader "RAG Paper," and aims for top-tier venues like ACL, EMNLP, or NAACL. It is also tracked by the AI Research Dashboard.

Development of the RAG Second Brain Paper commenced on February 8, 2026, with the most recent update recorded on February 10, 2026. The project is managed within Obsidian and involves Anirach in its development, while also being identified as an overarching project that Arthur works on. The paper has undergone significant revision and a multi-faceted development and versioning process.

Various versions of the paper exhibit different stages and statuses. Version v12 experiments have been completed, and the overarching project includes versions such as v15. Version 16 is the academic paper for which the RAG Second Brain Implementation project aims to provide experimental results. Version v23 was revised to address four CMT reviewer concerns, resulting in a version labeled 'v23 created with leakage-fr.' A subsequent version, 23.1, was submitted to the AIiH 2026 Conference on February 16, 2026, and is consistently referenced as the specific project paper submitted to this conference, maintaining an 'Active' status during its development. Concurrently, another version, v17 (also described as v17_final), achieved a "Complete" status on the same date, February 16, 2026, after confirming V3 experiment results, and was uploaded in PDF and .tex formats to Google Drive. The paper is scheduled for presentation at AIiH 2026.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific project paper, version 23.1, that was submitted to AIiH 2026.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific project paper, version 23.1, that was submitted to AIiH 2026.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific project paper, version 23.1, that was submitted to AIiH 2026.&lt;SEP&gt;A research paper, specifically version v15, that reports final results for Learned Gating using Sigmoid and Softmax R@10 metrics, compared against an RRF Baseline.&lt;SEP&gt;A specific project paper, version 23.1, that was submitted to AIiH 2026.</data>
