<node id="Pinned Tag">
  <data key="d0">Pinned Tag</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A Pinned Tag is a descriptive keyword or tag primarily employed to categorize and highlight specific content or documents. Its fundamental purpose is to indicate that the accompanying content has been "pinned." This "pinned" status signifies that the document or content is considered important, frequently accessed, or otherwise prioritized for enhanced visibility and streamlined retrieval. While often associated with Quick Reference documents, the application of a Pinned Tag extends to general content, allowing for the effective prioritization and easy identification of various types of information.&lt;SEP&gt;A tag indicating that content is pinned.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5a199a577367afc1799777199de40b72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aed742b710d4efe2e644f95efb224333&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90a4287e7a69589023f97fe208e724fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06fdd29b547fa50066bbf918525b3b2</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1772910997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Reference Tag">
  <data key="d0">Reference Tag</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">A Reference Tag is defined as a descriptive keyword, typically the term "reference" itself, used primarily for the categorization of documents and various other forms of content. Its fundamental purpose is to identify materials that are specifically intended to serve as references, with a particular emphasis on those designed for quick consultation.

This tag explicitly indicates that the associated content is provided for reference purposes. An illustrative example of a document that would be classified using a Reference Tag is the VPIPE Database Query Guide, which is categorized as a reference document.&lt;SEP&gt;The 'reference' tag is a keyword used for categorizing documents that serve as quick references.&lt;SEP&gt;A tag indicating that content is for reference purposes.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5a199a577367afc1799777199de40b72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e6c0f12f725a7b639074653a878c82f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aed742b710d4efe2e644f95efb224333&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90a4287e7a69589023f97fe208e724fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06fdd29b547fa50066bbf918525b3b2</data>
  <data key="d4">unknown_source</data>
  <data key="d5">1772910997</data>
  <data key="d6"></data>
</node>
<node id="Always-Load Tag">
  <data key="d0">Always-Load Tag</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">The Always-Load Tag is a descriptive keyword and a type of tag specifically designed to categorize content or documents. Its primary application is with Quick Reference documents. The fundamental purpose of this tag is to indicate that the associated content or document should consistently be loaded at the commencement of a session. By applying the Always-Load Tag, designated information, such as quick references, is ensured to be immediately available upon session initialization.&lt;SEP&gt;A tag indicating that content should always be loaded at session start.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-5a199a577367afc1799777199de40b72&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aed742b710d4efe2e644f95efb224333&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-90a4287e7a69589023f97fe208e724fd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a06fdd29b547fa50066bbf918525b3b2</data>
