<node id="Batch Source">
  <data key="d0">Batch Source</data>
  <data key="d1">concept</data>
  <data key="d2">Batch Source is a classification used to designate the origin or the specific method by which an action item was generated, created, sourced, introduced, categorized, or entered into a system. This entity provides critical information regarding the provenance of a given action item, specifying the particular process or system from which it emerged. It indicates the method of creation, often implying that the action item originated from a batch process or a batch-related source mechanism.

Specifically, Batch Source details the origin or method by which an action item was generated or added, confirming its introduction via a batch process into a system. This classification helps identify the source from which an action item was created, derived, or identified. It serves as the stated origin or method for numerous action items, including Action Item 133, Action Item 138, Action Item 109, Action Item 142, Action Item 113, Action Item 153, Action Item 132, Action-Item 152, Action-Item 135, Action-Item 150, Action Item 151, Action Item 125, Action Item 148, Action Item 158, and Item 156. Furthermore, Batch Source can specify the origin for action items with particular attributes, such as a 21.1KB action item, thereby confirming its generation through a batch-related method. For Action Item 158, the origin is explicitly identified as 'batch'.&lt;SEP&gt;The origin or source type from which Action Item 163 was generated.&lt;SEP&gt;The origin or method by which the action item was generated or identified.&lt;SEP&gt;The origin or method by which the action item was generated or added, specified as 'batch'.&lt;SEP&gt;The classification indicating that the action item originated from a batch process or source.&lt;SEP&gt;The stated source from which the action item originates.</data>
  <data key="d3">chunk-191ee41271401232e9471f34a4c06719&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b562d82caa30ba88bd5052e59263b9db&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2cca05ad3f3996f4bae901f9a8acc4bd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-74557fd16efa9a82777f3a28c0a487b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78ed7df389d60c81c051e05c8f353cff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-99d4a03820cfb4e612754c64db8aa7c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f5f9c416cabc71161b7cb94568b677f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a7d70bcf4d9abe91d1e8857f535f195&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5edcfa052169facf4607d180a7541573&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-367b61ad4d0cfbd9f210ee8fa52bdb2e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-564d8cbe6d4c74180f4e5a59066a30ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32564ea5df1261b524cbfe5099042e7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-acebf29512b1f172151cb83a3d9c8d0f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17260ee92d5f2ab447bb3a042a50f2a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2dce21d8f022f16327e246a33d693cf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-613e97c0cb32aa0139261992837483ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b1442862b96b3d5c9f6c0a383b69f83d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ae8eebcb1b490d870ca0689f9404763&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fb5787055cf4990acebdac313f83b3f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33488833b7907af8855976c04d1a45b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-88bf8df4772c8cb6a5b135c492da5a82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-42b7afbefee15f7ec80afb1bf6ff4c56&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0572c4108de149d5b983b26d5e40ed4a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a3e8441c4cefe10525b66c06522a219&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-744afc749372124945957d4a0fa3660b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b6f9007ed37a2d2015d2723fe141f45a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-23fda636ff43924bd38d40789fc5e489&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26ed990cda14504934345a280aafe273&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1d19890d1d093c09113388e12194d0cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9f1de4efc5e7b6ce50de1426a4a46cee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4eaae098a9fec8d05420eb0faf8f6104&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f28547105bf2d4243f6ae21da7501a82&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-df89a9a94938a9adcfad05fa14251c9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5a6d50cc87153f5563d210fdc8d86fe8</data>
